<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Segesta</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 241 BCE (approx.) · capital Segesta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Chief city of the Elymians of western Sicily, whose rivalry with Selinous drew both Athens and Carthage into Sicily.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Segesta was the main town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elymians">Elymians</a>, a people of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> who claimed Trojan descent; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> says they were Trojan refugees joined by Phocians. The city grew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-barbaro">Monte Barbaro</a> in the 7th century BCE and became increasingly Hellenised while keeping close ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/motya">Motya</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/panormus">Panormus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Segesta minted coins with Greek legends and wrote its own Elymian language in Greek letters, known from short inscriptions. Its unfinished Doric temple of the late 5th century BCE and its hilltop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> are among the best-preserved Greek monuments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Its long border dispute with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/selinous">Selinous</a> led it to seek Athenian help in 416 BCE, prompting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-expedition">Sicilian Expedition</a>, and then Carthaginian help, which ended in the destruction of Selinous in 409 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse">Agathocles of Syracuse</a> massacred many citizens in 307 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> Segesta went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, citing shared Trojan ancestry, and after 241 BCE it was a privileged free town of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. It was abandoned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/segesta.jpg" alt="Segesta" width="320" height="203"><figcaption>Image: Ludvig14 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Segesta_AncientGreekTemple_0932.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 241 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Segesta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Elymian and Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Elymian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Egesta, Elymian Segesta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-470&amp;focus=segesta" aria-label="Segesta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5000 814 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Segesta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5320 1070L5259 1106M5320 1070L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 1106h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5320 1070h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5320 1070h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-470&amp;focus=segesta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

